---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getAllGyms, getRegions } from '../lib/gyms.js';

// Counts come from the data, so this page cannot drift from reality.
const gyms = getAllGyms();
const priced = gyms.filter((g) => g.has_price);
const scraped = gyms.filter((g) => g.data_source === 'scrape');
const manual = gyms.filter((g) => g.data_source === 'manual');
const regions = getRegions();
const withDayPass = gyms.filter((g) => g.day_pass !== null);
const fourWeek = gyms.filter((g) => g.billing_period !== 'monthly');

const title = 'FAQ — Austin Gym Prices';
const description =
  'Where our gym prices come from, how often they are checked, what "all-in" means, and how to report a wrong price.';

const reportHref = 'mailto:reports@austingymprices.com?subject=Price%20correction';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">All gyms</a> / FAQ</nav>
    <h1>Frequently asked questions</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What this site is, where the numbers come from, and what to do when one of them
      is wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what">What is this?</h2>
    <p>
      A price comparison for gyms in Austin. We list {gyms.length} gyms across
      {regions.length} parts of the city and show what each one actually costs per month
      once enrollment and annual fees are counted, alongside contract terms and day-pass
      rates where they are published.
    </p>

    <h2 id="all-in">What does &ldquo;all-in&rdquo; mean?</h2>
    <p>
      The advertised monthly rate is rarely the whole cost. A gym might charge $29.99 a
      month and then add a $99 initiation fee and a $69 annual fee. Over a first year
      that is $44 a month, not $29.99.
    </p>
    <p>
      So every headline price on this site is the <b>all-in monthly cost</b>: the
      monthly rate for a year, plus any enrollment fee, plus any annual fee, divided by
      twelve. The advertised rate is still shown, inside the breakdown, because you
      should be able to see both.
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      This changes the answer, not just the presentation. At LA Fitness Anderson Lane the
      $29.99 plan costs more all-in than the $31.99 plan, because of the initiation fee.
      Sorting by sticker price would put the wrong one first.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Where do the prices come from?</h2>
    <p>
      From each gym&rsquo;s own website. {scraped.length} of the {gyms.length} gyms
      publish their rates publicly, and we read them from the gym&rsquo;s pricing page.
      Every gym page shows the date its price was last checked and the domain it came
      from.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other {manual.length} do not publish rates at all — mostly consult-gated
      chains and boutique studios that quote you in person. Those are collected by hand,
      by asking. Until we have a number we can stand behind, the gym shows
      <b>&ldquo;call for pricing&rdquo;</b> rather than an estimate.
    </p>

    <h2 id="missing">Why do some gyms have no price?</h2>
    <p>
      Because we do not have one we can source. {priced.length} of {gyms.length} gyms
      currently show a confirmed price. We would rather show a gap than a guess: a
      plausible-looking wrong number is worse than an obvious blank, because you cannot
      tell it is wrong until you are standing at the desk.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same rule applies to details. Where a gym does not publish its contract terms,
      no contract badge appears at all — we do not read silence as
      &ldquo;no contract&rdquo;.
    </p>

    <h2 id="freshness">How often are prices updated?</h2>
    <p>
      It depends on how often the gym changes them. Chains that run frequent promotions
      are re-checked weekly; stable independent gyms monthly. In January and June — the
      two months gyms actually change their offers — everything moves up a tier.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no site-wide &ldquo;updated daily&rdquo; claim here, because it would not
      be true for every gym. Each gym carries its own date instead. If a gym has not been
      re-checked in more than two weeks, its page says so plainly rather than presenting
      an old figure as current.
    </p>

    <h2 id="promos">What about introductory offers?</h2>
    <p>
      Promotional rates are shown as promotions, and the standing rate drives the
      comparison. A $1 startup fee that expires in three weeks should not make a gym look
      permanently cheap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where a gym publishes <em>only</em> a promotional price and never its standing rate,
      we say the price is not published rather than treating the promo as the real one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="billing">Why does one gym show a different price to the one on its own site?</h2>
    <p>
      Usually because of the billing cycle. {fourWeek.length === 1 ? 'One gym bills' : `${fourWeek.length} gyms bill`}
      every four weeks rather than monthly, which is thirteen payments a year, not twelve.
      A membership advertised at $215 &ldquo;a month&rdquo; on that cycle costs $233 a
      month in reality.
    </p>
    <p>
      We show the gym&rsquo;s own figure and its billing cycle in the breakdown, and use
      the true monthly equivalent for comparison. Nothing is silently converted.
    </p>

    <h2 id="daypass">Do you list day passes?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes, where the gym publishes one — {withDayPass.length} do so far. The Day passes
      tab switches the whole list to single-visit pricing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="wrong">A price is wrong. How do I report it?</h2>
    <p>
      Please tell us: <a href={reportHref}>reports@austingymprices.com</a>. Every gym page
      and every expanded card has a &ldquo;Report a wrong price&rdquo; link that fills in
      the gym name for you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Corrections are checked against the gym&rsquo;s own site and fixed. If you work at a
      gym listed here, see <a href="/for-gym-owners">for gym owners</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="affiliation">Are you affiliated with any of these gyms?</h2>
    <p>
      <b>No.</b> We are not owned by, paid by, or partnered with any gym on this site.
      Nothing here is sponsored, no gym has paid for placement, and there are no referral
      links or lead-generation forms. Ranking is by price and nothing else.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accuracy">Should I trust these numbers?</h2>
    <p>
      Check them before you sign. We read what gyms publish and we publish our sources and
      dates so you can verify any figure yourself — but pricing changes, promotions come
      and go, and staff quote things a website does not. Treat this as the shortlist, not
      the contract.
    </p>

    <p class="foot">
      Still stuck? <a href={reportHref}>Email us</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 34px 28px 90px;
  }

  .crumb {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  .crumb a {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .crumb a:hover {
    color: var(--orange);
  }

  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 2.8rem);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: 1.02;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-top: 14px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin: 40px 0 10px;
  }

  p {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .callout {
    background: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--orange);
    border-radius: 0 var(--r-sm) var(--r-sm) 0;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .foot {
    margin-top: 44px;
    padding-top: 18px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
